{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgPEQg2LmX5XNVd66xiA4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I graded F2 **\"true, not planted\"** (problem: null) and saved `grade.json`.\n\n- **Not one of the key's problems:** none of P1–P6 covers new-hire scrap. P6 is about the senior programmer and estimator leaving and the first-article failures that followed.\n- **But the truth confirms it:** the truth tables record a \"new hire\" scrap cause. It covers 4,005 scrap reports and 5,811 pieces over 2016–2025 (t430). It shows up in every year, between 269 and 1,058 pieces a year (t432).\n- **The labelling point also holds:** the finding said reason codes depend on who enters them. In the truth, 4,277 of the 5,811 new-hire pieces were coded \"operator error\" and none \"dimension out of tolerance\". Most out-of-tolerance scrap comes from true \"process\" causes (18,356 pieces) (t430).\n- **The size is low:** the finding's 353.8 excess pieces a year is the right effect, but smaller than the truth's average of about 581 a year (5,811 over 10 years, t432). So its $18,914 a year probably understates the cost.\n\nOne small gap: I didn't separately check its claim that scrap isn't concentrated in particular parts or product families. The grade rests on the confirmed new-hire mechanism."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":526,"cache_read_input_tokens":31726,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":526},"output_tokens":430,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad7e93e8-b876-410d-8301-b751aabda607","uuid":"2601935f-fe41-485c-9b0b-56aaaf73f020","timestamp":"2026-10-25T15:58:51.736Z","request_id":"req_011CgPEQfjVWmHSFVeeFJzcW"}
